#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176603} :activity/id #uuid "a54a0eb8-1c10-55bc-94b1-373798ccde02" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048244 :topic/id #uuid "8ba6899a-6ac7-5c57-b387-cdf9a5dd7846" :topic/math-academy-id 3548} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176603 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177063 :tutorial/content "Recall that we can use the second derivative to determine the concavity of a differentiable function $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if $f''(x) > 0$ on $(a,b),$ while\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on an interval $(a,b)$ if $f''(x) < 0$ on $(a,b).$\n\nSo, to determine intervals of concavity of $f(x)$ by looking at the graph of $f'(x),$ we need to pay attention to where $f'(x)$ is increasing or decreasing.\n\n- If $f'(x)$ is increasing, then $f''(x)>0,$ which means $f(x)$ is concave up.\n\n![](images/8a/8ac780a2dfa4d6c32449b750ff236568764de920d38e363d1badf4c7324c9c94.png)\n\n- If $f'(x)$ is decreasing, then $f''(x)<0,$ which means $f(x)$ is concave down.\n\n![](images/2b/2bcc5e92916f582dc75450241c0223b55dab287d24e06b55e4d8fde56c7f927c.png)\n\nAlso, recall that $f(x)$ has an inflection point at $x=a$ if $f''(x)$ changes its sign around $x=a.$ So, to determine the inflection points of $f(x)$ by looking at a graph of $f'(x),$ we need to pay attention to where $f'(x)$ switches from increasing to decreasing (or vice versa). \n\nIn other words, we need to pay attention to relative extrema on the graph of $f'(x).$\n\n![](images/68/68a0a5523690eceb319bff14916528b6c1727d68e393373553bc42fbf6be6f16.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "8ef5e0fb-85b8-5d0c-890a-85fa61aaef1a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 649 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f8c6b188-b62b-5303-8c70-a1b3b79fe9c2" :step/math-academy-id 1241 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176673}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176604 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175900 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175765 :question/id #uuid "403f94fd-793b-55eb-a7c4-14f97cf4ca79" :question/math-academy-id "e-338"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cda8b343-e71d-5ec2-ae82-3f66dba09264" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176501} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516781} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516841}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Given the Graph of a Derivative"} :step/id #uuid "f16dbb41-7982-5668-b0bb-22d6b038dbe3" :step/math-academy-id 1242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176673 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175947 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175796 :question/id #uuid "7a25d851-2f79-5e70-bde2-eda99697fe3e" :question/math-academy-id "e-6851"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f73c50f7-fa8c-5d23-8699-e7e074f4251b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049604}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176574} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516833} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516857}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Inflection Points and Intervals of Concavity Given the Graph of a Derivative"} :step/id #uuid "71c365fe-68f2-5852-89cf-41fb104a5949" :step/math-academy-id 25381 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176604}}] :activity/title "Interpreting the Graph of a Function's Derivative: Concavity and Points of Inflection" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176100}]}
